\deflang1033\pard\plain\f2\fs22\cf0 Summary:  This bill would accomplish one of the first steps required by the original Planned Growth Strategy Ordinance.  The PGS Ordinance calls for the adoption by the Council of an Infrastructure and Growth Plan to guide future decisions related to impact fees, the CIP and transportation land use measures.  The definition of the Infrastructure and Growth Plan, as defined in the original PGS ordinance, is a forecast of population, housing and employment for the City and County for 2010 and 2025, and is broken down into 14 subareas.  The Council directed in the original PGS Ordinance that the Infrastructure and Growth Plan be made up of the forecast in the PGS Report after being modified and updated by MRCOG forecasts for 2025.  MRCOG prepared the updated numbers using the assumptions set forth in the PGS Ordinance.  The PGS Implementation Advisory Task Force reviewed those numbers and requested that the forecasts be "rerun" with less emphasis on filling vacant property throughout the City, and more emphasis on redeveloping property along the designated Centers and Corridors (as defined in the Comprehensive Plan.)  The two forecasts were similar, so the PGS Implementation Advisory Task Force recommended that both "scenarios" be sent to the Impact Fee Committee with a copy sent to the Council.  The Exhibits attached to the bill include the numbers from both scenarios by showing the range between the two scenarios.  The bill requires that the Infrastructure and Growth Plan be reviewed every 3 years.                
\par 
\par Financial Impact: This bill has no financial impact, however, as the first step in the PGS implementation process, it should set into motion actions to be taken by the City that will have financial impact.
\par 
\par Comments: The Council should review the Infrastructure and Growth Plan tables and decide whether to adopt them as presented  -- with a range of forecast outcomes \endash  or to choose one scenario over the other.   As stated above, the forecasts are intended to be reviewed periodically and modified, if necessary.  The bill requires one amendment to insert the population forecast (Exhibit 1).  The Council should consider another amendment that would include language from the original PGS Ordinance about the relationship between the I & G Plan and impact fees.
